since it first released in 2014 I've had  a love-hate relationship with destiny at  its best it gave me thrilling co-op  strikes and multiplayer raids it also  felt better than most shooters I had  played with such fluid aiming and  satisfying gunplay that those games just  felt off in hindsight but for me and  many others there was also a dark side  to destiny aside with pointless missions  and wasted playtime frustrating loot  drops and constant disappointment the  taken King expansions somewhat rectified  these problems with a revamped  progression system but the late game  still lacked enough variety and reward  to make it feel like the hours we poured  into it were well spent with destiny -  developer Bungie yet again made strides  in some areas while digressing in others  early game loot was parsed out at a  better pace structured around a wider  variety of quests than its predecessor  had but still the RNG cloud hung over  everything faction rallies infuriated  players iron banner events never  guaranteed the items you wanted as was  always the case loot drops were more a  product of random numbers and what felt  like luck Bungie has patched its sequel  several times allowing players to buy  the specific items they want but much of  it feels like it's too little too late  the bottom line is the more I play  destiny the less assured I am that my  time will be worth it too much of it  feels up to chance  destiny demands that you spend a lot of  time with it but doesn't guaranteed that  that time will be a good investment for  me this paradox recently reached a  boiling point I fell off the destiny  train so to speak with so many other  games releasing I couldn't justify  pouring hours into the sci-fi shooter  every day or even every week yes the  games mechanics are airtight and as a  fan of shooters I have to tip my hat to  Bungie and I don't judge anyone who has  stuck with destiny - since its release  but within this sci-fi universe  I never really felt in control of the  outcome and barring any major overhauls  - the way destiny to inherently  functions I decided to avoid this type  of game and buy this type of game I'm  talking about lifestyle games  the YouTube channel extra credits  defines these as titles you play more  than any other games you make friends  through games you play on a regular  basis over long periods of time they  often have daily chores to accomplish  they can come from any number of genres  from MMORPGs to MOBAs sports titles to  shooters  these are games you keep returning to  time and time again sometimes out of  habit in recent years we've often seen  games less as discrete experiences and  more as services to me destiny is the  prime example ubisoft's the division  also aimed to scratch that itch and  based on everything we've heard so far  it seems like Bioware xantham will  follow in the same vein but before all  of these games existed there was monster  hunter if you've been playing since the  series beginning in 2004 then you know  how much of a lifestyle game each  Monster Hunter entry can be but as  someone who only just stoven to the  franchise with monster hunter world I  was astounded to find many of the same  characteristics as the aforementioned  games chief among them being destiny and  what's more I was taken off-guard by  just how many things Monster Hunter does  better  I know I just said I was swearing off  this type of game but hear me out  although Monster Hunter is designed to  be a time sink and many of its quests  and events will likely become repetitive  over hundreds of hours it actually gives  you control over much of the outcome yes  there is RNG involved with certain item  drops and limited time events but  throughout my time with Monster Hunter  World I never feel as if I'm wasting it  that's because the game embraces an idea  that destiny and the division rarely do  agency for instance I want to craft the  irathient armor set so I set out to hunt  a Ratheon to complete the set I need a  Ratheon spike an item that sometimes  won't drop and in this case it didn't  this was bad luck  what sets monster hunter apart is the  fact that I can greatly increase my  chances of getting that spike next time  by severing the creatures tail it gives  me more control than I might otherwise  have in destiny I would have repeated  the same strike or raid boss shooting at  it ad nauseam until it died hoping it  would drop the armor piece I need it yes  you can pop a three of coins in the hope  of buffing your chances at better loot  but you never really know if your  efforts will come to fruition in Monster  Hunter your intentions mean much more  the same rings true with armor and  weapon crafting unlike in destiny you're  not waiting for the weapon you want to  drop with the exact modifiers you need  in monster hunter you set your goals you  hunt the intended monsters gather the  necessary parts and you control the  weapon modifiers you work for  furthermore our monster hunters focus on  agency results in more chances to  prepare for upcoming assignments I can  eat specific meals for certain stat  boosts I can outfit my palico cat  companion with stronger gear I can equip  myself with the armor most resistant to  the next monster is bleeding attacks I  can also accept the most useful bounties  for the hunting grounds I'm heading into  ensuring I'll have upgrade points to  spend on existing armor or to reinforce  new ones this brings me to my next point  in Monster Hunter very few rewards feel  useless while destiny supplies a  constant slew of boots  robes weapons gauntlets and class items  most of it will be cast aside or  converted into some sort of currency in  the late-game stages in monster hunter  world however that Rado bond armor can  remain useful in the later hours  especially when you upgrade it to its  alpha or beta forms allowing for gems  that increase certain resistances aiding  you in your fight against the high-level  opponent this all results in as my  coworker John Lucas said a much blurrier  line between the early game and  late-game  in destiny and the division the first  half felt like a completely separate  experience from the latter in monster  hunter world it all feels cohesive once  again conveying the feeling that every  hour a spend will contribute to my goals  I'll use numerous load outs after dozens  of hours I'll swap between weapon types  as I hunt new monsters I'll gather herbs  for potions and bugs to make traps it  all feels worthwhile because it all  encourages self-improvement I'm not just  grinding to reach the endgame but  enjoying the journey there in the first  place the control freak and me  appreciates monster hunters habit of  respecting my time  it makes destiny and the division feel  almost obsolete in their current forms  if I'm going to pour days or weeks into  a single game I want to know what values  my effort I may have sworn off these so  called life style games for a while but  Monster Hunter world has me hooked and  it's nice to not feel exploited  [Music]
 

